    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Owners now have a `riskFlag` field, and it covers two of your three conditions. The \"disposable-adjacent\" email check isn't in yet because nothing in the code or API spec defines it, and I didn't want to make up a rule. The full test suite passes (31 tests, including the new `riskFlag` test).\n\n**What I changed:**\n- `src/main/resources/openapi.yml`: added a read-only `riskFlag` boolean to the Owner schema.\n- `OwnerMapper.java`: added `toRiskFlag(owner)`. It returns true when the owner is a possible duplicate or the city-capacity warning was set when the owner was created; otherwise false.\n\n**Decisions for you:**\n\n1. **\"Disposable-adjacent\" email domain.** The app already rejects the three blocked domains (`mailinator.com`, `tempmail.com`, `guerrillamail.com`) and their subdomains outright. So a saved owner can never have one, and \"adjacent\" must mean something else. Possible meanings:\n   - lookalike domains, e.g. `mailinator.net` or `tempmail.co`;\n   - a separate \"suspicious but allowed\" list of domains;\n   - something else you have in mind.\n\n   Tell me the rule or the domain list and I'll add it, with tests.\n\n2. **\"Soft capacity.\"** I used the existing capacity warning, which is set when the city already had 40\u201349 owners before this one (the hard limit is 50). If \"over its soft capacity\" should strictly mean more than 40 rather than 40 or more, that's a one-line change.\n\nOnly the \"normally false\" case has a test so far. I can add tests for the duplicate and city-capacity cases once you've answered on the two points above.",
